`code-atlas watch` decides whether another process changed the project configuration by looking for the phrase "project configuration changed" inside the free-text reason of a degraded freshness report. Rewording the engine's message would silently stop watch from reloading, and nothing else can tell why the freshness barrier is degraded without parsing English.

Replace the free-text reason of a degraded freshness report with a typed cause. `FreshnessReport::Degraded` should carry `cause: DegradedCause` instead of `reason: String`, with `DegradedCause` exported from `atlas_engine`:

- `ProjectConfigChanged`: another engine adopted a different project configuration;
- `UpdateFailed { error: String }`: the barrier could not bring the index up to date (the error's message);
- `PartialRun { reason: String }`: the index run finished partial (the run's own reason, unchanged);
- `FilesNotIndexed { count: usize }`: otherwise degraded because `count` files or repositories could not be indexed.

`DegradedCause` implements `Display` as "project configuration changed; reopen the engine", "index update failed: {error}", "{reason}" and "{count} file(s) or repositories could not be indexed" respectively. Query responses (CLI and MCP, text and JSON) keep reporting a degraded state with a textual `reason`, which is now the cause's `Display` text. That matches today's wording in every case except one: a successful run that still had failed files used to say "some files or repositories could not be indexed" and now reports the count like the other files-not-indexed path. `watch` must recognise a configuration change from the typed cause. Update everything that builds or reads the degraded report, including the existing tests and the documentation.

Bug report: Code Atlas stopped linking our web client to endpoints that live on Express routers nested more than one level deep across files.

Our API is laid out like this:

```ts
// src/server.ts
import express from 'express';
import { apiRouter } from './routes';
import { healthRouter } from './health';
const app = express();
app.use('/api', apiRouter);
app.use('/health', healthRouter);

// src/routes/index.ts
import { Router } from 'express';
import { ordersRouter } from './orders';
export const apiRouter = Router();
apiRouter.use('/orders', ordersRouter);

// src/routes/orders.ts
import { Router } from 'express';
export const ordersRouter = Router();
ordersRouter.get('/:id', (req, res) => res.json({ id: req.params.id }));
```

After indexing, there is no composed endpoint `GET /api/orders/{id}`; only the router-relative `GET /{id}` exists, and the web app's `fetch(`${process.env.STORE_API_URL}/api/orders/${id}`)` gets no `calls_endpoint` link at all. Routers mounted directly on the app from another file (`app.use('/health', healthRouter)` with routes in `health.ts`) still compose and link correctly, so it is only the deeper nesting that is broken.

Find the root cause and fix it there (not with a workaround downstream), and add a regression test at the level where the bug lives. Keep the existing tests green.

`serveStatic({ precompressed: true })` only serves a precompressed variant when the client lists the coding as a bare token. Real browsers and CDNs send quality values, and then we silently fall back to the uncompressed file:

- `Accept-Encoding: gzip;q=1.0, br;q=0.5` with `app.js.gz` and `app.js.br` on disk serves the plain `app.js`.
- `Accept-Encoding: br;q=0.2, gzip;q=0.8` should get gzip, the client's preferred coding, but gets no compression at all.

Please make the precompressed lookup negotiate properly, the same way Hono already negotiates `Accept-*` headers elsewhere:

- pick the available variant with the highest quality value;
- a coding with `q=0` must never be served, and `*` covers every coding that is not listed explicitly;
- when quality values tie, prefer br, then zstd, then gzip (today's order);
- coding names are case-insensitive; the `Content-Encoding` we send stays lowercase;
- if the preferred variant does not exist on disk, fall back to the next acceptable one, and serve the original file if none is acceptable.

Plain lists such as `gzip, br` must keep working exactly as today, including the `Vary: Accept-Encoding` header. Add or update tests for the new behaviour.

Environment note: dependencies are already installed. The global `pnpm` shim on this machine cannot run the pinned pnpm 12.6.0, so use `npx pnpm@12.6.0 ...` or the binaries in `./node_modules/.bin` (for example `./node_modules/.bin/vitest run --project main <files>`).
